### Telegram channel config
```
"channels.telegram": {
  "dmPolicy": "allowlist"|"disabled"|"allow",
  "groupPolicy": "allowlist"|"disabled"|"allow",
  "defaultAccount": "<account-name>",
  "accounts": {
    "<account-name>": {
      "botToken": "${ENV_VAR_NAME}",
      "dmPolicy": "allowlist",
      "capabilities": { "inlineButtons": "all"|"dm"|"group"|"off" },
      "allowFrom": ["<user_id>", ...]
    }
  },
  "groups": {
    "<chat_id>": { "requireMention": false, "allowFrom": [...] }
  }
}
```
- DMs default disabled. Allow per-account via `dmPolicy: "allowlist"` + `allowFrom`.
- `bindings` array routes incoming messages to agents. Pattern: `{type:"route", agentId, match:{channel:"telegram", accountId, peer:{kind:"direct"|"group", id:"<chat_id>"}}}`. `peer.kind="direct"` requires `peer.id` to be the user_id.

### Don't poll a bot's getUpdates while OpenClaw is polling it
Telegram allows ONE active long-poll per token. Any external `curl getUpdates` causes 409 "Conflict: terminated by other getUpdates request" and the gateway has to retry with backoff. During the conflict window, real user messages can be missed or delayed.
- For diagnostics, look at the gateway logs instead of probing getUpdates
- If you must probe, deactivate the bot in OpenClaw config first (`enabled: false`) and restart

